WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented musician

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.003
and actress
you know from her work

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.105
on shows like "younger"
and "Lizzie McGuire."

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:09.075
She both stars in and produces
"How I Met Your Father."

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.243
The second season begins
streaming on Hulu tomorrow,

00:00:11.243 --> 00:00:13.212
with new episodes available
on Tuesdays.

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:14.581
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.615
-Why don't you
go grab us some drinks

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:18.051
and snag us a table?
-Oh, sure.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:19.852
Uh, what do you want to drink?
-Just surprise me.

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:23.356
Listen, everyone,
I don't want Ian to know

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.124
that I've burned through
two different guys

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:26.359
in the past day.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.161
So everyone be cool and chill,
just like me.

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:31.297
Cool and chill, yeah?
Let me hear you say it.

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:33.466
What am I?
-Cool and chill.

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:36.769
-Alright. Now let's do me.
I am tall and curious.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:40.339
-Please welcome back to
the show Hilary Duff, everybody!

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.574
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.574 --> 00:00:45.578
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:55.822
♪♪

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:58.691
Welcome back!
I'm so happy to have you.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.193
-Thank you so much.
-Hi, Hilary!

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.994
-Ah!
-There you go. Look at that.

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:03.129
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Ah.

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.430
-Last time,
you were here on Zoom.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.098
The audience couldn't react
with the same enthusiasm.

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:07.733
-You didn't give me that
on Zoom.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.169
-No, I didn't.
-No.

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:11.905
-I think I was like, "Couldn't
make the trip, huh, Hilary?"

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:14.507
[ Laughter ]
I do want to check in with you

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:15.941
because you were saying,
last time we spoke,

00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:18.911
that you had taught yourself
to play guitar a little bit.

00:01:18.911 --> 00:01:20.680
You taught yourself
a couple songs

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.214
people always really ask for
at parties --

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:24.083
the "Batman" theme and
the "Mission: Impossible" theme.

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.118
-I'm really popular at parties.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:28.588
-She goes, "Who wants to hear
the 'Batman' theme?"

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:31.056
-Yeah, you know
what's really, really --

00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:33.926
I knew were gonna ask me this,
and if you

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:36.095
put a guitar in my lap,
I couldn't do it anymore.

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:38.198
I decided to have another child.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:41.767
-Crushed all my personal dreams.
-So you actually had two kids.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:43.670
You were like, "Am I gonna be
a professional guitarist

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.072
or have a third child?"
-Yeah. We chose --

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.341
-That's lovely
that you chose family.

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:50.143
That's really sweet.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:53.146
We also -- I wanted to check in
because your middle --

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.481
your daughter, wild child.
-Yeah.

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:57.383
-Is she still?
-You know what?

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.520
She's -- I don't want to say
she's a wild child,

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.622
because she's not bad.
She's just, like,

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.657
the things
that come out of her mouth

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.292
and the way
that her brain thinks,

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.894
she's like a unicorn.
She's just --

00:02:08.894 --> 00:02:10.930
-Which is wonderful.
-She surprises us all the time.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.033
-Our middle one is the same way.
-Is that a middle-child thing?

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.335
-Maybe it's a middle child.
Our youngest --

00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:17.670
and I think your youngest
is the same way --

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.138
it's a little too early
to tell exactly.

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:20.339
-Yeah.
-But we feel like

00:02:20.339 --> 00:02:22.809
our youngest is wired
the way our oldest is,

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.611
and the one in the middle is
just on his own frequency.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.479
-I feel the same way.
I feel the same way.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:31.417
The first and the third are
just, like, a little calmer.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:34.453
Like, they can just -- just be,

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:38.157
and Banks is, like, on the couch
like, existing.

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.993
And actually, we're all just
existing around her orbit.

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:43.262
You know?
-My middle one is doing

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:45.932
a thing recently
where he'll scream "Daddy,"

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.701
and I'll run in, and then --
-That never happens

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:51.037
in my household.
-Oh, really?

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:53.339
Nobody -- They just have, like,
given up on the dad showing up?

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.541
-Well, it's not -- He's there.
-He's there,

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:57.042
but he's not a problem solver?
-And he's like, "I'm

00:02:57.042 --> 00:02:59.245
perfectly willing and able."
It's just they all want me.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.680
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.582
-Well, look at me.
-I mean, look at me.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:04.550
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:05.751
-He'll say "Daddy,"
and then I'll run in,

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:07.886
and he'll be playing
with, like, toy men.

00:03:07.886 --> 00:03:10.723
And he'll be like,
"No, not you. His daddy."

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:13.158
So he's, like, play-acting
with toy daddies.

00:03:13.158 --> 00:03:15.761
And then there's
only so many times

00:03:15.761 --> 00:03:18.130
you can hear him scream "Daddy,"
and, like, you're sitting there,

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.299
and you're like,
"It's not me. It's the toy."

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:22.101
-Meanwhile, he's, like, bleeding
in the room next door.

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:23.870
-I went in one time, and
he had a massive head injury.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.237
No.
-Oh, my God.

00:03:25.237 --> 00:03:27.339
[ Laughter ]
Oh, my God.

00:03:27.339 --> 00:03:29.142
-You did -- This is --

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:31.210
Again, having three kids,
I know the feeling.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.479
And your holiday card,
you really leaned in

00:03:33.479 --> 00:03:35.548
to how chaotic
the three kids can be.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:39.618
Here, you guys are basically
wrapped up like hostages.

00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:42.622
-Yeah.
-With Christmas tree lights.

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.523
Now, do the kids enjoy --
I mean, obviously

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:46.392
they understand what
the staging here is,

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.661
is that they are basically --
you're captives to their whims.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:51.230
-I mean isn't that
the generic theme

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:52.998
running through
our whole entire life?

00:03:52.998 --> 00:03:54.400
-I guess so,
but do the kids know?

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.935
Like, when you're like, "Hey,
we're gonna take a picture

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:57.103
where you guys
look like maniacs,

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:59.938
and we're, like, living
as your prisoners,

00:03:59.938 --> 00:04:01.574
and they're like,
"Yeah, that sounds right."

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:04.177
-Yeah, Luca was aware,
and he was really into

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:07.380
tying us up
in the Christmas lights.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.248
Banks was just thrilled
to have a cupcake.

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:10.850
And same with Mae.
Sorry.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.086
Mae was happy to have a cupcake,
and Banks was like,

00:04:14.086 --> 00:04:15.988
"Do I get a treat after this?
Great."

00:04:15.988 --> 00:04:17.924
-I will say, that is very smart
to actually have

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:20.860
eatable cupcakes on-set.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.628
I mean, the rug went to [Bleep]
afterwards.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:24.764
-Yeah.
You didn't think the --

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:27.800 line:38%
I really enjoy
"All is not calm".

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.134 line:38%
-Yeah.
Did you see the top of --

00:04:29.134 --> 00:04:31.571
I was really proud of the top.
We have a blended family,

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:33.939
so you've got to kind of
just make light of it.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:36.041
-Uh-huh.
-Duffs and Bairs and a Comrie.

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:38.878 line:76%
There's lots of last names
in our household, so...

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.313 line:78%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:43.315
-You -- This is Banks
outed you.

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:45.685
-Oh, Seth, are you gonna
do this to me?

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:48.421
-Well, you put it --
You can't put it on social media

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:52.024
and then be like, "Oh, that was
a secret I told everyone."

00:04:52.024 --> 00:04:53.258
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.258 --> 00:04:55.461
-I do that sometimes, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.596
I'm actually excited
to say "diarrhea" on your show

00:04:57.596 --> 00:04:59.298
as many times as possible.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:00.833
-I'm gonna start right now.
-That was the first one.

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.902
-You share the story.
-You did say

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:05.370
Banks told a story at camp.
"My mom made bad chicken,

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.373
and now she has..."
-Diarrhea.

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:08.607
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:10.242
-That's what happens
when you make bad chicken.

00:05:10.242 --> 00:05:12.945
What I like is, what happened
at soccer camp

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:14.847
that she thought, "I actually
have something

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:16.949
that will add
to the conversation"?

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:19.518
-I don't think
she was prompted at all.

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:23.222
I think she just walked up --
By the way, her soccer coach,

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.258
very good-looking man.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:27.459
-Very good-looking man.
And I was at work.

00:05:27.459 --> 00:05:31.163
Thank God I was at work.
But my nanny called

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:34.867
and said, "Well, now,
the whole entire soccer class

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:37.537
and coach knows
that you ate bad chicken.

00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:39.104
You made the bad chicken,
and now you're

00:05:39.104 --> 00:05:40.707
paying for the bad chicken."
-Yeah.

00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:42.642
[ Laughter ]
-Like, not cool, Banks.

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:45.211
And she was like,
"My mom has diarrhea."

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:46.479
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:48.247
-To be honest, it's
really a double burn,

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:50.082
because, one, you have diarrhea.
You don't want people --

00:05:50.082 --> 00:05:51.384
-I wasn't having a great day.
-But also it's like,

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:53.886
"My mom has diarrhea,
and it's her fault."

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.255
-Yeah, yeah, and,
"She undercooked the chicken

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:58.458
and almost poisoned us all."
No one ate it but me.

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:01.159
[ Laughter ]
-Congratulations on the show.

00:06:01.159 --> 00:06:03.996
A really cool thing happens --
happened, I should say --

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:05.531
which I feel like
doesn't happen in TV anymore.

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.233
You did a 10-episode
first season,

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:09.568
and it went so well and
people liked it so much,

00:06:09.568 --> 00:06:11.137
you're doing
a 20-episode season now.

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:14.206
That's very old-school,
the 20-episode sitcom season.

00:06:14.206 --> 00:06:16.943
Was it really rewarding
when you all got the news,

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:18.611
"Hey, we're gonna do
twice as much"?

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:21.613
-Oh, gosh. I mean,
you know how this business is.

00:06:21.613 --> 00:06:22.881
You get a phone call,
and you're just like...

00:06:22.881 --> 00:06:24.483
-Yeah.
-"Are we getting picked up?

00:06:24.483 --> 00:06:26.385
Are we getting fired?
Are we getting hired again?"

00:06:26.385 --> 00:06:27.953
I don't know what the --
you know, you're kind of always

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:30.622
on the edge of your seat.
And to get that news,

00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:32.258
it was really exciting.
You know?

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:33.959
We knew the numbers
were really good

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:37.396
from the first season,
but to double-order the show

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:39.531
and just give us
that extra creative space

00:06:39.531 --> 00:06:42.702
for the story lines
to just, you know, dive deeper

00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:44.136
into each character
and get to have

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:47.139
a little bit more fun and
bring on, you know, more people,

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:50.042
it was -- it was really, like,
just --

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:52.411
you just sign on to something,
hoping it works.

00:06:52.411 --> 00:06:55.114
And it was working.
-It's really nice.

00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:56.516
It's really exciting
to be on the upswing.

00:06:56.516 --> 00:07:00.653
You mentioned adding new people.
Uh, John Corbett.

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:02.154
-John Corbett, yeah.
There he is.

00:07:02.154 --> 00:07:04.657
-John Corbett,
love interest now.

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:06.758
Also played your music teacher
a long time ago.

00:07:06.758 --> 00:07:10.329
-He sure did.
And now we are smooching it up.

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:12.732
-This would have been very bad
if that happened --

00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:15.301
-Very, very bad.
It did not happen then.

00:07:15.301 --> 00:07:18.103
-A lot of time passed,
and then it's an age difference

00:07:18.103 --> 00:07:20.105
that everybody
has got to be cool with.

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:22.041
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
I mean, she's 35 now.

00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:23.643
-Yeah, it's totally fine.
-Cool.

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:27.246
-Are you
a "Sex and the City" fan?

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:29.247
-I mean, yes. Aren't we all?

00:07:29.247 --> 00:07:31.283
-Yeah. I should say --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:31.283 --> 00:07:32.951
I'll be honest -- late convert.

00:07:32.951 --> 00:07:34.787
I sort of missed it
when it happened.

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.789
You know, my wife, Alexi,
made me watch it all,

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:38.657
and totally bought it.
Loved it.

00:07:38.657 --> 00:07:40.359
-I mean, happy wife,
happy life, right?

00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:42.060
-Yeah, exactly.
But I should note,

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.562
I was also happy
when I watched it.

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:46.532
I wasn't just like --
-No, it's a forever goody.

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:49.268
But also, like, in my mind,
he's Aidan, so...

00:07:49.268 --> 00:07:52.404
-Yeah.
-I did call him John on-set,

00:07:52.404 --> 00:07:54.173
but I have to suffer --
not suffer through.

00:07:54.173 --> 00:07:57.009
But, you know, a lot of my life,
like, I'm called Lizzie.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:59.145
So he's Aidan.
That's what it is.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.546
-If he complained
about it,

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:02.014
you're like,
"Buddy, get in line."

00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:03.482
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.482 --> 00:08:04.950
-What a delight
to have you back.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.253
Thank you so much for being
here, and do come back anytime.

00:08:07.253 --> 00:08:09.721
You guys, give it up
for Hilary Duff.

00:08:09.721 --> 00:08:13.025
The second season
of "How I Met Your Father"

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:14.326
begins streaming tomorrow
on Hulu.

00:08:14.326 --> 00:08:15.528
We'll be right back
with Kim Petras.

